Guillaume Dargaud <noUSEspNETam@gdargaud.net> wrote:
> Well, thanks to Michal's great advice, I got my new 2.4.9 kernel 'mostly'
> running on my LX164 with RH7.2, except for one this: the network card, a
> Digital 21140 Ethernet.

Tulip driver is somewhat temperamental.  It works or does not depending
on a hardware (apparently there is over 100 different variants with
various quirks), what is at the other end of a wire (hub?, switch?
model?), a phase of the moon, a number of sacrificed black chickes,
other things ...

In summary it is known that 'tulip' has serious troubles with
autonegotiations but maybe more.

> - there seems to be two sets of drivers for it, the DE4X5 and the TULIP.
> Which one should be used ?

The one which works.

> - does they need to be compiled as part of the driver or as external module

Does not matter but modules are easier to deal and experiment with;
especially if you have to pass some parameters.  Switching monolithic
driver means recompiling your kernel.

BTW - with 2.4 kernels and my "network environment" I have to use
'de4x5' as with 'tulip' driver no single packet was ever send.
With 2.2 kernels 'tulip' driver worked dandy and 'de4x5' had troubles.


> - tulip as part of the kernel, no modules: it doesn't compile. I get plenty
> of messages about multiple definitions in tulip_old.o and tulip.o. I guess
> one is enough...?

You cannot have both built-in at the same time.  One or another.  You
can have both as modules.

> - tulip as external module. When I do "insmod tulip" I get a "insmod: tulip:
> no module by that name found", so I guess I need to compile it somehow...

Then you do not have a module or it is not correctly installed.
BTW - use 'modprobe tulip' or 'modprobe -v tulip' instead.
Read documentation to find out why.

> /etc/conf.modules has "alias eth0 tulip"
> and /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-eth0 seems also properly
> configured.

So?  It is really listed by your 'modules.dep'?

> Any clue...?

'man modprobe', 'man mii-tool'.

I strongly suspect that 'de4x5' has better chances but if you are
loding 'tulip' first (as a module or as a built-in) then 'de4x5'
cannot be loaded.  Therefore modules are easier as you can always
'rmmod ... ' to try another one.

   Michal


