This means that ethernet packets travel on a wire _both_ ways
hence the whole thing is basically functional.

> - the alpha seems to have some trouble seeing the outside. It can ping
> itself (localhost), but nothing else (by either IP or hostname).

That is exactly backwards then "usual" network trouble situations. :-)

Possible reasons for that that come to mind (not necessarily in that
order):
  - broken ping binaries
  - "funny" firewall rules
  - blocked ICMP packets (some or all - shudder!) on some router in
    between
  - messed up routing
  - some other network misconfiguration (duplicate IP numbers?)
  - ....

If you have a root access to another machine on the same network as your
Alpha then try 'arp -en', or similar, on both ends to see what is in arp
tables after a ping attempt.  To really see what is going on on a
network you have 'tcpdump' (if you have X running then 'ethereal' is
easier to use and interpret).  'strace ping ...' may also, sometimes,
give you some information.

  Michal


