I feel your pain.  My UP2000 died a week ago today.  :-(

First thing to do is get the debug monitor up to see what's
going on with the processor, memory, floppy, SRM, etc.  The
UP2000 has a jumper that brings up the debug monitor instead
of trying to bring up SRM or AlphaBIOS.  If you're lucky,
the UP1000 will have something similar.  Useful commands
include (IIRC) 'memtest', 'flload', 'romboot', 'flboot', 'date',
'help', and one whose name slips my mind that shows you the
headers of what's in flash.  To use 'fl*', you will need a
good SRM on a floppy (FAT formatted, IIRC).  If you're really
lucky, this will be enough to install a good SRM and get you
up and running.  (There may be another jumper that protects
the flash from being written.  You will need to enable write
before attempting to burn SRM into flash.)

The debug monitor on the UP2000 can run from the serial port
or from the graphics card and keyboard.  With luck, the UP1000
has an option in that department that works for you.

In my case, the UP2000 debug monitor tested memory flawlessly,
read the floppy SRM, verified the flash and floppy SRMs were
identical, and everything else _except_ boot into SRM.  I'm
told those symptoms mean the motherboard is bad and needs to
be repaired.

If the board is less than 3 years old, it may still be under
warranty.  Microway in Plymouth, MA handles warranty and other
repairs on API motherboards.  Mine arrived there yesterday for
non-warranty repair.  Fortunately, I believe my credit card
will pick up the repair bill of a few hundred dollars.  Of
course, some boards cannot be repaired.  :-(

Good luck.

