Douglas H. Quebbeman <dougq@iglou.com> wrote:
> 
> Thus my ignorance: how would I know what style partition
> tables I've got?

Type 'fdisk -l /dev/sda' or whatever other disk you are checking.
It does not say explicitely but output is distinct enough for
that to be clear (this is not the only way to find that out).
Basically if you partitions are named "/dev/sda1" and similar
then this is FAT style partitioning.  If they are marked with
letters from "a" to "h" then you have a BSD type.

> I take it there's no hard-fast linkage
> between the type of partition table, and the types of the
> filesystems chosen for the drive?

There is no such relationship whatsoever.  Filesystem do not care
about partition table at all.  You may erase the later, restore
it, replace by another type, and filesystems and your data will
be not affected (you may have serious troubles to get there after
a reboot if you will not do that carefuly enough not to mess
anything but partition table and recreate correct partition data
but your file systems will be present).  Really this is mostly a
firmware which you are using to boot your machine which depends
on some particular format.  As a corollary on your non-boot
devices you can use any kind of partitioning your kernel
recognizes.  Also a kernel could get an information about
partition boundaries by other means than reading info from
initial disk sectors (and this will likely happen in not so
distant future).

  Michal


