Personally I find systems which will not boot because / is
messed up, or /tmp is overflowing, or there are obscure bugs in
a bootloader, or ... a much more serious PITA. :-)

'/' itself is fairly static so chances that something will go wrong
there are rather small and a system is bootable even if you lost
everything under /usr.  It is even possible to run with '/' mounted
read-only although the later requires some "creative hacking".  The
problem is that various things try to write in /dev, /etc and /root
and not always you can ignore that.  AFAIK so far this applies to
all Linux distributions.

> /var, /usr, /etc, /opt, /usr/local/,
> /tmp, etc. all have different space needs that may change unexpectedly. So I
> just lump them together as /, maybe use a /boot and /home, and if I need a
> new partition add a new disk (since all my space on the other one is already
> available).

You can partition "root", "boot" and "the rest mounted on /usr" if you
so desire.  Go wild with journals, etc.  Various directories can be
provided as symbolic links and/or "bind-mounted" (although one should be
careful with the later as installer so far does not know about 'bind'
which may lead to screwups during updates).  There is also a possibility
of using LVM but I have no idea how good or bad this fares on Alpha.

Separating "system stuff" from "here are users" makes also much
easier to do reasonable backups.

> Lots of partitioning is useful if you want to prevent out-of-control
> processes from stomping your root partition

"Small root" does not not imply "lots of partitions" and you can even
move things like /var and replace them with links after an initial
installation.  You do not really need /var to boot into a single-user
mode.

   Michal


