make bootpfile works just fine,  don't forget to tell the kernel where the
local root is, passed as a boot flag  example:  -fl "root=/dev/sda2"

so the boot would look like:    boot  -proto bootp  -fi "mykernel" -fl
"root=/dev/sda3"  ewa0
(assuming ewa0 is your network adapter)

make sure ewa0_protocols is set to  bootp, not mop  (else if there's a
secondary bootp issued, it may get issued as a mop load instead of bootp..)

do a show ewa0*    at the srm console prompt.  and verify your network card
is set to bootp.

JJ



