Err... you don't as you may only have "...=y", or "...=m", or "not set".
Assuming that you really mean the later are you really compiling your
kernel for an embedded operation or for a serial console only?  As
otherwise you will not see very much.  Documentation/Configure.help
says:

  You need at least one virtual terminal device in order to make use
  of your keyboard and monitor. Therefore, only people configuring an
  embedded system would want to say N here ....

If you installed 'kernel-source' package then grab a fitting
configuration file from a subdirectory 'configs' there, copy it over
.config in your work directory, edit that _removing_ all lines where
you would like to change in that configuration (these would be likely
only those which refer to your disk driver which you want to be
built-in and to EXT3/JBD which you want built-in and not as modules
because you will not use initrd).  Leaving ext3 as a module will
make your / to mount as ext2 - which is possibly what you actually
want.  This is not for me to say.  After that run 'make oldconfig'
and answer _few_ questions.  Leave other customizations to a time
when you will really know that you want them.  Getting something
workable is the first priority.

> I searched groups.google but no relevant information...

Using 'grep -r' on your sources would likely be more revealing.

