>Support for all SCSI controllers in Red Hat 7.2 distribution is
>loaded as modules (if you are using distribution kernels, of course,
>and not custom ones) from initrd. 'mkinitrd' utility builds
>an appropriate image for a given kernel.  It checks at least 
>/etc/modules.conf and /etc/fstab to guess what should be included
>but you can pass your wishes also from a command line.
>
>> The installer placed a initrd image in sda1:/boot which will very
>> likely include the needed driver.
>> Unfortunately, AFAIK MILO can't handle seperate (i.e. not the same file as
>> the kernel) initrds and there's no SRM available for XL and XLTs.
>
>Eh?  If that would be the case then the whole installation would not be
>possible in the first place.

There's the possibility of appending (or was it prepending) a ramdisk or
initrd to the actual kernel image, therefore allowing bootloaders which
doesn't know about seperate images to work. That's what the kernel
parameter ramdisk_start is for.

>                              In any case 'initrd=...' parameter is
>interpreted by your _kernel_ and not by milo.  Most likely a typo in a

You're wrong here. There's no initrd= parameter to the Linux kernel, it's
interpreted by some boatloaders and others (like MILO) don't interpret it.
Have a look into /usr/src/linux/init/main.c for parsing of commandline
options or at /usr/src/linux/Documentation/initrd.txt which states just the
same.

>boot command.  It is very easy to get one character wrong (and that is
>why milo.conf is a nice thing to have).  On a distribution CD there are
>some sample boot lines in etc/aboot.cnf.  They are not exactly like
>those for a finished installation and with milo there are some small
>differences but the idea is the same.

One of the main differences is, aboot knows about initrds, but MILO doesn't.

cu, Bodo
