Eric S. Johansson wrote:

> is the magic phrase.  Maybe I should just load up srm(?) instead.  in
> any case, I do appreciate all your suggestions.

Well, it is definately possible to get SRM to work on an LX164. I have it
running just fine on mine and if you want to boot Unix it is definately
much better than that ARC crap, but....

The process of swapping from one to the other is not at all easy, primarily
because although there are lots of instructions available on both the
Compaq (now HP?) web site and google groups, they all say different unclear
things and none seems to be completely correct or written to address this
particular common situation.  To be frank, I have no idea exactly how I did
it myself.  I copied all the files I could find onto a multitude of
floppies and kept bunging them in.  I do seem to recall the following:

1) I couldn't go straight from ARC to the latest SRM, but had to go via an
older SRM.  Perhaps I was just doing something wrong.

2) When you boot SRM you often end up with a blank blue screen and conclude
that it is not working.  This is caused by it defaulting to a serial
console.  There is a key sequence which will get a prompt (Ctrl-P ?) and
then you can change a setting so that it defaults to the video card in
future.

3) It IS possible to boot and LX164 from IDE under SRM despite what it says
in many places about this being impossible.

4) Don't forget that you need to reformat your hard disc to use BSD
partitions tables.

5) Once you get it working it is worth the pain.  It certainly boots much
faster.

I would like to go back to the old BIOS and then find an easy route to SRM
and document it, but I am scared that I couldn't get back, and if the
server stops working for any length of time I get serious ear ache from her
indoors!!!

Good luck,

        Richard Simpson


