 >>There should be a way to boot a rescue system on the CD, which may
 >>have tools to fix whatever is broken if it is fixable.
 >
 > Yeah, but what tool ? I can't seem to find a proper e2fsck...

[Some of this is explained in the RELEASE_NOTES that appear on the
1st CD-ROM of the Phoebe RHL8.0.92 beta.  I had to do it after installing
Phoebe onto a system that also has another root with RHL7.3.]

Boot "linux rescue" from the 1st CD-ROM of the newer distribution,
and do not mount the old root.  If RHL7.2 doesn't let you
skip the mounting of the old root, then use RHL8.0, which does.

For each ext2 or ext3 filesystem, see what features it uses:

	tune2fs -l /dev/...   |  grep features

To revert to ext2, you must remove all features except "filetype" and
"sparse_super".  In particular, the "has_journal" feature is the
difference between ext2 and ext3, and the "dir_index" feature is
new for RHL8.1.  Use

	tune2fs -O ^has_journal /dev/...
	tune2fs -O ^dir_index /dev/...    # if needed

Then check+repair each filesystem using the newer e2fsck:

	e2fsck -f /dev/...

Then boot "linux rescue" from the 1st CD-ROM of the older distribution,
and try to avoid mounting anything.  Check+repair each filesystem using
the older e2fsck.  Then you should have no trouble from the "has unknown
features" part of booting the old system.  [You may still have trouble
if critical files were lost or scribbled, etc.]



