Quite unlikely.  You do not really need much space in / and it is
a very good idea to have rather small, separate / and possibly also
/boot.  One good reason for that is that they you can format these
as ext2 with '-b 1024 -O none' so you are not dependent on the
"latest and greatest" bootloaders but you can use whatever you
know that it works for you.  On "work" partitions you can then
use any supported filesystem you wish.

> I can't boot from the RedHat installer CD 'cause it stops if the filesystems
> are unclean.

How you are booting?  If from SRM then boot with '-fl 4'.  A comment
in 'etc/aboot.cfg' on CD says:

#   4 - Boot the Red Hat Linux installer in text only rescue mode

You will get a shell and enough of a system to fix things.  Sometimes
it is necessary to unpack bits and pieces from CD into a ramdisk.
Updates which failed in the middle are, unfortunately, nasty situations
as things are inconsistent all over and it is hard to tell what
will work and what will require some unconventional hacks.

If you are using milo you have to figure out a way to do something
equivalent.

> Any way to compile the source (I couldn't find it) ?

Sources are on one of CDs (if you made the whole set) but it is
nice to a have a system on which to compile. :-)

