I find over-partitioning to be a *real* problem, and have given up on trying
to pre-plot where things will wind up. /var, /usr, /etc, /opt, /usr/local/,
/tmp, etc. all have different space needs that may change unexpectedly. So I
just lump them together as /, maybe use a /boot and /home, and if I need a
new partition add a new disk (since all my space on the other one is already
available).

/boot, OK, separate if you want.

Lots of partitioning is useful if you want to prevent out-of-control
processes from stomping your root partition insted of your website or ftp
site or whatever, but in general I find it to be more trouble than its worth
to have more than /, /boot, and maybe /home or /scratch.




