It's only "evil-ly proprietary" if it requires using programming
techniques that make it difficult/impossible to deploy code on other
architectures.

I don't see this being a huge problem; it's not as if people will be
instantly leaping into writing great gobs of code to this end.
Consider some common applications that /won't/ have any intrinsic
support for this:

- Apache tries to take advantage of threading, but they surely won't
  be rewriting it just for HyperThreading(tm);

- Ditto, to varying extents, for PostgreSQL, SAP-DB, Interbase,
  Oracle, and other DBMSes;

- How much impact with HyperThreading(tm) have on SAMBA?  NFS?
  Perl?  Python?  

It's hardly going to have frightening evil effects if taking advantage
of it requires reimplementing /everything/ from scratch, which no one
considers even vaguely feasible.
