On Fri, 07 Feb 2003 23:45:39 +0200, moonshine_religion wrote:
> What is you point? As the world waits for generation 3 Itanium to
> fulfill the promise of the failed generation 1 or 2 for that matter,
> other technologies exists. While Hyper-Threading is an interesting
> technology it is as proprietary as any other technology and therefore
> not in the best interest of the industry. The promise of Hyper-Threading
> is also yet to be seen. How many processor developments have be touted
> as the "next revolution in processor design" by the marketing folks only
> to fail to live up to that promise in the real world. Itanium?

This is an Alpha vs. Itanium thread, in the comp.os.linux.alpha newsgroup,
right? :) My point is that Alpha architects have once again been ahead of
their time with SMT. EV8 was supposed to be 4-way, while AFAIK Intel's HT
implementations are 2-way.

SMT is all about hardware. You don't have to change your code or something
to make it run on an SMT-enabled processor (this was referring to posts
further down the thread). My point is that Intel is branding a technology
based on the RnD efforts of the Alpha team. And since this _is_ an "Alpha
vs. Itanium" thread, I'd go with the Alpha. The reason why "the industry
is holding it's breath" for Itanium is that Alpha is dead (yeah yeah... I
know... there will be an EV78 and an EV79... but that's about it), and
Itanium seems to be the most (if not the only) viable 64-bit multi-purpose
microprocessor architecture. I don't know what AMD's Hammer will do when
it comes out of its lair... I just feel it's not right to abandon such
really magnificent superscalar RISC archs, as the SGIs, SUNs, and Alphas,
for something that was, until recently, considered entry-to-mid-level
(i.e. x86).

cya,
Nick.

