"Dr. David Kirkby" <drkirkby@ntlworld.com> writes:

> I am keen to test the software on other operating systems. For some
> reason (related to the IEEE standard for floating point I believe),
> Dec Alphas seem more fussy about code (especially floating point) so I
> am keen to get one as a test bed for the software.

Note that different generations of Alphas behave different here
performance-wise. The 21064 and 21164 cannot do math with "funny"
numbers like Inf and NaN. They can generate them, though. So if you
try, say, Inf+Inf, you'll get a floating point exception, unless you
compile with special compiler flags (which will slow down normal
operations). Then the kernel is able to fix it up when it occurs, but
the code will crawl like hell. The 21264 FPU can do this all itself,
so the performance impact isn't that large.

Another "problem" is uninitialized variables; because of the
aforementioned property, Alphas might throw an exception if an
uninitialized value contains a trap representation. Be sure to make
your compiler warn about uninitialized values.

